<p>
<font size=5>A Look at Turbidity and Streamflow</font><br>
<b>Steve Bauer</b><br>
<i>Pocket Water, Inc. (PWI)</i>
<p>
Turbidity and stage is measured continuously in Red River about midway on the WMA property near the abandoned bridge.  Since the data loggers were installed on June 24th, the river stage has dropped abruptly from a high of 3.4 feet to 1.9 feet as of July 21st.  These river levels correspond to discharge levels of approximately 750 cfs and 110 cfs, respectively.  The high of 3.4 feet is above the rating curve for the station so the flow estimate is provisional until further is made by comparison to nearby gauging stations.
<p>
Precipitation data is obtained from the weather gauge located near the Red River Ranger Station.  The National Weather Service posts recent data from this gauge on the Internet, while more historical records are posted by the Western Regional Climate Center.
<p>
During rainstorms, turbidity fluctuates with discharge (see figures below).  Heavy rains of over 1/3 of an inch on July 3rd increased turbidity from 4 NTUs to 8 NTUs.  This level is still quite low compared to the turbidity levels associated with the construction project.  The background information is useful to provide a framework for evaluating project-related turbidity.
